{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 CRA basic personal amount & how tax is calculated, Finance Canada (tax expenditures, lowest-rate report), T4127 (2026) \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three friends each get the same one thousand dollar tax break."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Nina saves eight dollars. Omar saves two hundred and ninety-six. Priya saves four hundred and thirty-four."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Same thousand dollars. Three different results."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Change one word, from deduction to credit, and the results change completely. By the end of this video, you'll know which one is worth more to you, and why."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers: twenty twenty-six, Ontario, employment income. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: where each one works on your return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Your return runs in a fixed order. Income first. Then deductions. Then the tax. Then credits."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A deduction comes off your income, before the tax is calculated. An R R S P contribution is the classic example."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A credit comes off the tax itself, after it's calculated."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That one difference in order changes everything."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, deductions."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A deduction saves you whatever tax that last thousand dollars of income would have cost. That's your marginal rate."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The higher your income, the higher that rate, and the more a deduction is worth."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Nina earns fourteen thousand dollars. Her tax for the whole year is about eight dollars. So a thousand-dollar deduction saves her eight dollars. There's nothing more to save."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Omar earns sixty-five thousand. The same deduction saves him two hundred and ninety-six dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Priya earns one hundred and twenty thousand. She saves four hundred and thirty-four."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The Department of Finance says it plainly: deductions give bigger savings to people with higher marginal tax rates."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, credits."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Most credits on your return are non-refundable. You claim an amount, and it's multiplied by the lowest tax rate."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Federally, that's fourteen percent in twenty twenty-six. In Ontario, five point zero five percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So a one thousand dollar credit amount is worth about one hundred and ninety dollars and fifty cents. For Omar, and for Priya. Exactly the same."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A credit doesn't care how much you earn. As long as you owe enough tax to use it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And that's the catch."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Here's the part most people miss. A non-refundable credit can only bring your tax down to zero. Anything left over is lost."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Nina owes eight dollars. So her thousand-dollar credit amount is worth eight dollars to her. The rest disappears."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For someone with little or no tax, a non-refundable credit and a deduction can both be worth almost nothing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "But there's a third kind: refundable credits."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A refundable credit is paid to you even if you owe no tax at all. The C R A treats the income tax already taken off your paycheques the same way."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's why low-income Canadians should always file. Refundable credits and many benefits depend on filing a return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So which is worth more? Let's put it side by side."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Nina: the deduction, eight dollars. The non-refundable credit, eight dollars. A refundable credit, paid in full."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Omar: the deduction, two hundred and ninety-six. The credit, one hundred and ninety dollars and fifty cents. The deduction wins."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Priya: four hundred and thirty-four, against the same one hundred and ninety and fifty. The deduction wins by more."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Once your income is past the first bracket, a deduction is usually worth more than a credit of the same size."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And there's a bonus. A deduction lowers your net income. That number also decides income-tested benefits, like the Canada child benefit and the O A S clawback."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "A credit doesn't touch net income at all."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, what can you do with this?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One: if you have a choice of timing, take deductions in your higher-income years. R R S P deductions can even be carried forward to a later year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two: for couples, many credits can be claimed by either partner. Give them to the partner who has enough tax to use them."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three: with little or no tax, focus on refundable credits and benefits, and file every year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Where this changes. Your exact marginal rate depends on your province and your income, and some deductions and credits have their own limits. Your return does the final math."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's your checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Know your marginal rate: that's what a deduction is worth."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Know the lowest rate: that's what a non-refundable credit is worth."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Check whether you owe enough tax to use your credits."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. File every year, even with no income."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Deductions come off income. Credits come off tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. A deduction is worth more as income rises. A credit is worth the same, if you can use it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Only refundable credits pay you with zero tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: why a tax refund doesn't mean you paid less tax. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
